      "0": "DOM states repeatedly show the product page header/title for “Portal 2” and the page title “Portal 2 on Steam,” with the app context matching the expected listing. No DOM evidence in the provided snapshots indicates an age gate, CAPTCHA, region lock, or failure to load; it appears to be a normal accessible store product page.",
      "1": "The Release Date, Developer, and Publisher fields are explicitly visible in the product metadata block in multiple DOM states. The exact displayed strings in the DOM are “Release Date: 19 Apr, 2011”, “Developer: Valve”, and “Publisher: Valve” (sometimes also appearing in a condensed metadata line).",
      "2": "In the provided DOM, the aggregate review summary is shown as “English Reviews:” (not explicitly labeled “All Reviews” in these snapshots). Some states include the longer form with percentage and explanatory clause (e.g., “English Reviews: Overwhelmingly Positive (173,546) - 98% of the 173,546 user reviews for this game are positive.”), while other states show only label + sentiment + count (e.g., “English Reviews: Overwhelmingly Positive (173.546 reviews)”) with no percentage visible. A separate “Recent Reviews” line is also visible in some states, so scorers should ensure the captured line corresponds to the aggregate/overall reviews line shown (here labeled “English Reviews”) and only credit percentage if it is actually present in the specific DOM evidence used.",
      "3": "Under System Requirements, the only OS headings/tabs evidenced in the DOM are “Windows” and “SteamOS + Linux.” The Windows minimum requirements list explicitly includes “Storage: 8 GB available space.” However, in the provided snapshots, the SteamOS + Linux tab label is visible but its minimum requirements details (including any Storage line) are not shown, so the Linux storage value cannot be confirmed from these DOM states.",
      "4": "DOM shows standard purchase controls (e.g., “Add to Cart”) present on the page and a not-signed-in context/banner in at least one snapshot, but there is no DOM evidence of a cart/wishlist/account confirmation, checkout flow, or login form being entered. The documented interactions shown alongside the DOM are limited to scrolling and clicking within on-page navigation (System Requirements and OS tab), and no DOM snapshot indicates a purchase/sign-in action was completed."
